>1) How thoroughly do you search each dungeon level before continuing
>to the next?

Differing levels in different parts of the game. In the early game I
search pretty completely. I usually speed run through the Mines until I
hit the bottom. Then I do a more leisurely search heading back up.

Generally once I get through the Quest levels there's more than enough
stuff around not to necessarily search the entire level. 

In Gehennom it's just finding the up and down stairs.

>Do you try to discover all secret rooms (including shops
>and vaults)?

Shops always. Once you get telepathy it takes only a couple of turns to
ID a shopkeeper or Priest in a temple. So again while it's necessary to
search the upper dungeon, the lower dungeon doesn't really have that
same need.

>Also, how many times do you search one spot before
>deciding nothing's there (using "n[number]s")?

Generally I'll 10s a spot.

>2) Is "leveling" a good idea in nethack, e.g. running around until you
>see a monster to kill (to gain experience)?

Big debate. As an advocate of the Minetown Protection Racket, I beleve
that controlled levelling in the early game can be beneficial. Others
think that the benefits of levelling up far outweigh the downsides. It's
up to you find a style that fits you. The points I'd like to add is
that in the early game you can level up your pet to XL9 without
impacting game balance. There are also several ways to improve your hit
points without gaining too much experience in the early game.

By the time you can enchant armor and have enough money to buy
protection, the point is moot. It's really only a discussion in the
early game as to how fast to level up.

>3) Do you make the effort of picking up every single item, even if you
>don't need it, in order to sell it in a shop - unless the load would
>burden you, or course?

No, unless it's a really pricey item (like plate mail for example). The
reason is that shops already have lots and lots of items for you to sell
to the shopkeep. Plus your equipment gives you a lot more. Pets are your
best friends in shops. Use them to clean the shop out and the shopkeep
of his cash.

>Even more, do you run back for items you left
>because you couldn't carry any more items (for the same purpose of
>selling them)?

I stash to keep weight down, not for selling. A bag of holding is one of
my coveted early mid game items. I generally hope that the prize at the
top of Sokoban is in fact a BoH.

